  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates to: 'very low probability of winning nothing.' The individual focuses only on the low chance of receiving 0 Euros in Lottery A (1% probability). There is no mention of other outcomes, probabilities, or comparisons of averages. The justification centers solely on the unlikelihood of the worst outcome. No consideration of 'probable outcomes' as defined by the reason (i.e., those above the 1/n threshold) is evident, nor is there any indication of averaging such outcomes. The deliberation is narrow and risk-avoidant regarding the zero outcome, but does not engage with the structure required by the decision reason.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying outcomes above a probability threshold (based on number of outcomes) and averaging those. The individual does not categorize outcomes by a systematic threshold or compute averages of such outcomes. Instead, the focus is only on the 1% chance of getting nothing, which is not sufficient to infer use of the probable-outcome averaging rule. The justification in the report does not align with the rule of the decision reason. There is no evidence that the individual identified 'probable' outcomes in the way the reason defines them or performed any averaging. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

